Gefunden beim Abarbeiten von karr #112. Vorbestehend, unabhaengig vom dortigen Guard.

Jeder Baum-Walk in dieser Distribution rekursiert ohne no warnings
"recursion": _sorted_leaves, _encrypt_tree, _decrypt_tree, _document_leaves,
_assert_acyclic, _expansion_census, Encrypted::_canonical_floats. Perls
Schwelle von 100 ist fest, also warnt ein Dokument, das tiefer als 100
verschachtelt ist, bei JEDEM Aufruf jenseits davon -- einmal pro Knoten,
pro Walk.

Gemessen an einer 265 Ebenen tiefen Alias-Kette (ein Dokument, das sops
akzeptiert): File::SOPS->encrypt braucht 0,77 s und schreibt 6,4 MB, dabei
in der Groessenordnung 100.000 Warnzeilen auf STDERR, verteilt auf
_sorted_leaves, _encrypt_tree und Encrypted::_canonical_floats. Werden die
Warnungen ueber $SIG{__WARN__} eingesammelt statt verworfen, dauert
derselbe Aufruf mehr als 2 Minuten -- die Warnungen kosten mehr als die
Arbeit.

Kein Datenverlust und kein Wire-Problem: die Ausgabe ist korrekt. Es ist
Diagnose-Laerm, der eine korrekte Operation wie einen Fehler aussehen
laesst, und er trifft genau die tiefen Dokumente, die sops akzeptiert.

Offene Frage vor einem Fix: no warnings "recursion" lokal in den Walks
schaltet auch das Netz ab, mit dem t/41 seinen Rueckfall begrenzt. Die
beiden Interessen kollidieren, also nicht blind stumm schalten -- t/41
liest die Warnung absichtlich (und t/43 dokumentiert, warum sie dort
gerade nicht greift).
DONE, with one part handed to karr #120.

MEASURED FIRST, because it decided the design: sops DOES have a depth limit.
Against sops 3.13.3, counting containers from the documents own root mapping,
same boundary for `a: {a: {a: ...}}` and `a: [[[ ... ]]]`:

  go-yaml (sops -e AND sops -d)  10001 accepted, 10002 refused
                                 "yaml: exceeded max depth of 10000"
  encoding/json (sops -e)        10000 accepted, 10001 refused
                                 "invalid character { exceeded max depth"

and the -d refusal precedes the key, like the cycle and the alias bomb: the
same over-deep encrypted file with SOPS_AGE_KEY_FILE=/nonexistent still reports
the depth. So a depth bound is reference-compatible, and 10000 is the deepest
a document can be and still be readable in BOTH formats.

DECISION (docs/adr/0029), three parts that only work together:
  1. `no warnings recursion` in the six walks in SOPS.pm, per walk not per file.
  2. $File::SOPS::MAX_DEPTH = 10000, asked by every one of those walks. This is
     what replaces the warning as a signal, and it is a real guard rather than
     a test hook: a document too deep to process is now refused instead of
     crawling. Writable, so t/41 can lower it -- and so can a caller reading
     untrusted documents.
  3. The walks carry ONE key path, pushed and popped, instead of copying it per
     level. Without this the bound is decorative: 4000 levels took 101s and
     410 MB in _sorted_leaves alone, 10000 never finished. With it, 10000
     levels take 0.017s and 24 MB.

NUMBERS (the tickets own two claims did not reproduce on HEAD, and are corrected):
  265-level alias chain, encrypt: 505 warning lines / 63,457 bytes STDERR /
  0.807s BEFORE -- not ~100,000 lines. With a collecting $SIG{__WARN__} it was
  0.754s, not >2 minutes. The 6.4 MB in the ticket is the encrypted OUTPUT, and
  that part reproduces exactly (6,411,482 bytes). The huge numbers most likely
  predate the karr #112 alias-bomb guard, which now refuses the shapes that
  cross perls threshold tens of thousands of times.
  AFTER: 168 lines / 23,856 bytes / 0.417s, and every remaining line is
  File::SOPS::Encrypted::_canonical_floats plus Format::YAMLs two leaf walks --
  karr #116s files, off limits here. Handed over as karr #120.

t/41s NET, re-verified as a net: the child now lowers $File::SOPS::MAX_DEPTH to
200 instead of dying on perls "Deep recursion" warning. Rehearsed twice against
the final tree -- cycle guard body removed: 14 of 23 red in 0.367s; guard call
sites removed: 14 of 23 red in 0.363s. Every failure reports RAN AWAY, none
hangs, none allocates. Shape-independent, which the warning was not for the
leafy cycles: a plain bound at the default 10000 would have cost 2.5 GB there.

NEW t/45-deep-document-is-quiet.t (37 tests, 6.4s): the noise itself, the bound
